      "text": "To solve this problem, we can use the ratio of girls to boys, which is 3:2, and the total number of students, which is 45. We can set up a proportion to find the number of girls in the class.\n\nLet's call the number of girls in the class G and the number of boys in the class B. We know that the ratio of girls to boys is 3:2, so we can write the following equation:\n\nG/B = 3/2\n\nWe also know that the total number of students is 45, so we can write another equation:\n\nG + B = 45\n\nNow we have a system of two equations with two variables. We can solve this system by using the substitution method. We can solve the first equation for G:\n\nG = (3/2)B\n\nNow we can substitute this expression for G into the second equation:\n\n(3/2)B + B = 45\n\nTo solve for B, we can combine the terms on the left side of the equation:\n\n(3/2)B + (2/2)B = 45\n\n(5/2)B = 45\n\nNow we can solve for B by multiplying both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of (5/2), which is (2/5):\n\nB = (2/5)(45)\n\nB = 18\n\nNow that we know the number of boys in the class is 18, we can use the ratio of girls to boys to find the number of girls:\n\nG = (3/2)B\n\nG = (3/2)(18)\n\nG = 27\n\nTherefore, there are 27 girls in Ms. Snow's math class.",
